- Public research: the American Library Association and the University of Maryland used SpeedOf.Me as the mobile speed-capture tool in their [2015 IMLS-funded study](https://www.ala.org/sites/default/files/tools/content/BroadbandQuality-SpeedTestHighlights.pdf) of broadband in 2,251 public library locations across 49 states.
- Textbooks and study guides: Pearson's Technology in Action (the chapter 7 "Testing Your Internet Connection Speed" exercise, in every edition since the 13th, 2016), Cengage's Digital Technologies for the Australian Curriculum 9 & 10, the Wiley/Sybex CCST Cisco Certified Support Technician Study Guide (its own figure) and O'Reilly's Defensive Security Handbook (2nd ed.). A ready-to-assign lab exercise and answer key for instructors, with standing permission to reproduce SpeedOf.Me screens in course materials, is at [For Educators](https://speedof.me/for-educators).
- Reference: [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speedof.me) (English and Italian) and [Wikidata Q17052323](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q17052323).

Several of the research papers score every test against a saturated multi-stream peak. SpeedOf.Me deliberately measures a single stream on the open internet and reads lower on that ruler by design ([Real-World Speed](https://speedof.me/real-world-speed)), so cite the research as "documented in" or "analyzed in", not as a validation of accuracy. None of the organizations above is a partner; each citation is that organization's own editorial, support or research choice.

